nodecode-crontab is a rewrite of olric3015's olric3015/hermes-plugins:

  https://github.com/olric3015/hermes-plugins
  commit c5a412f46ab5cc14bb82528481a46c8f8bb3fe2a
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 olric3015 (LICENSE)

Rewritten: the whole reading. The plugin put a `@crontab:` reference in front of
the model at the point a message was composed; a Nodecode cell has no such
reference seam, so the same reading is an `explain_cron` tool the model calls
and a `/crontab` command the operator types, both answered from one body. The
expression grammar and its expansion, the sentence in plain words, a row per
field, the notes (day of month OR day of week, `*` in the minute field, a step
that does not divide the hour or the day, days 29-31, 7 as Sunday), and the
next-runs arithmetic were carried over; croniter became masks and a day scan
written here, and the zone is read out of the machine's own tz database (the
TZif transitions) rather than through the host runtime. The plugin's shortcuts,
`L` and the nth weekday `5#3`, and the optional 6th (seconds) and 7th (year)
fields are all still read. The section -- a timezone, the tz database's folder,
and how many runs a reading lists -- is the cell's config, which the plugin had
in a runtime timezone setting.

Left out: the line about Hermes's own `/cron` check ("Hermes /cron: accepted as
a cron expression"), which repeats a guard in the Hermes runtime (`cron/jobs.py`'s
field pattern) that a Nodecode cell does not have -- the cell reads the
expression itself and says what it means, and points at the cron cell for
actually scheduling something. Also left out: the TUI and desktop composer
suggestions, which belonged to a Hermes front end. The `explain_cron` tool,
`/crontab` and the `crontab` help topic are additions, not part of the original.
